data.content:
Since there's no `.csproj` yet, here are the two lightest options:

### Option 1 — .NET 8+ `TimeProvider` (zero new types)

.NET 8 ships an abstract `TimeProvider` class. You accept it as a parameter and call `GetUtcNow()` instead of `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`. In tests, pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`.

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider clock)
{
    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

```csharp
// test
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

`TimeProvider.System` is the production default.

### Option 2 — Any TFM: ambient `Func`

If you're pre-.NET 8 or want zero dependencies:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    internal Func<DateTimeOffset> Clock { get; set; } = () => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => Clock() > dueDate;
}
```

Tests override `Clock`; production code never touches it.

---

**Recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, go with `TimeProvider` — it's built-in, well-known, and `FakeTimeProvider` (from `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing`) handles advancing time, timers, etc. Want me to apply either approach to your file?
